                ],
                "remediation": "Authorize the caller at the start of the entry point, before any storage read or write that depends on the caller. Call `require_auth` or `require_auth_for_args` first, then perform the state change.",
                "severity": "medium"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authorization after state mutation"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A public contract entry point narrows an integer with `as`, where the operand comes from a function parameter or a storage read. In Rust an `as` cast between integers truncates the high bits and wraps instead of reporting an error, so an out-of-range amount, timestamp, or counter can become a small unintended value. Widenings and same-width casts are ignored. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Unchecked integer cast\n\nA public contract entry point narrows an integer with `as`, where the operand comes from a function parameter or a storage read. In Rust an `as` cast between integers truncates the high bits and wraps instead of reporting an error, so an out-of-range amount, timestamp, or counter can become a small unintended value. Widenings and same-width casts are ignored. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\n### Remediation\n\nUse fallible conversion: `u32::try_from(value)` / `value.try_into()` and handle the error with a typed contract error. Reserve `as` for casts whose range has already been checked.\n\n### References\n\n- https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#type-cast-expressions\n- https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/trait.TryFrom.html",
                "text": "A public contract entry point narrows an integer with `as`, where the operand comes from a function parameter or a storage read. In Rust an `as` cast between integers truncates the high bits and wraps instead of reporting an error, so an out-of-range amount, timestamp, or counter can become a small unintended value. Widenings and same-width casts are ignored. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\nRemediation: Use fallible conversion: `u32::try_from(value)` / `value.try_into()` and handle the error with a typed contract error. Reserve `as` for casts whose range has already been checked."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#type-cast-expressions",
              "id": "SS-018",
              "name": "Unchecked integer cast",
              "properties": {
                "category": "arithmetic",
                "confidence": "low",
                "references": [
                  "https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#type-cast-expressions",
                  "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/trait.TryFrom.html"
                ],
                "remediation": "Use fallible conversion: `u32::try_from(value)` / `value.try_into()` and handle the error with a typed contract error. Reserve `as` for casts whose range has already been checked.",
                "severity": "medium"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unchecked integer cast"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A contract entry point decodes caller-supplied bytes or XDR (`from_xdr`, `from_bytes`, or `try_from`) without checking the input length or handling a fallible decode result. Malformed or attacker-chosen payloads can yield unexpected values, panic the contract, or bypass validation that was assumed to happen during decoding. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Unvalidated XDR or bytes deserialization\n\nA contract entry point decodes caller-supplied bytes or XDR (`from_xdr`, `from_bytes`, or `try_from`) without checking the input length or handling a fallible decode result. Malformed or attacker-chosen payloads can yield unexpected values, panic the contract, or bypass validation that was assumed to happen during decoding. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\n### Remediation\n\nValidate the payload length before decoding and handle the decode result explicitly with `match`, `?`, or a fallible helper that maps errors to a typed contract error. Reject trailing bytes and values outside the expected range.\n\n### References\n\n- https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/trait.TryFrom.html\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security",
                "text": "A contract entry point decodes caller-supplied bytes or XDR (`from_xdr`, `from_bytes`, or `try_from`) without checking the input length or handling a fallible decode result. Malformed or attacker-chosen payloads can yield unexpected values, panic the contract, or bypass validation that was assumed to happen during decoding. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\nRemediation: Validate the payload length before decoding and handle the decode result explicitly with `match`, `?`, or a fallible helper that maps errors to a typed contract error. Reject trailing bytes and values outside the expected range."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/trait.TryFrom.html",
              "id": "SS-020",
              "name": "Unvalidated XDR or bytes deserialization",
              "properties": {
                "category": "error-handling",
                "confidence": "low",
                "references": [
                  "https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/convert/trait.TryFrom.html",
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security"
                ],
                "remediation": "Validate the payload length before decoding and handle the decode result explicitly with `match`, `?`, or a fallible helper that maps errors to a typed contract error. Reject trailing bytes and values outside the expected range.",
                "severity": "medium"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unvalidated XDR or bytes deserialization"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Temporary storage entries are removed when their TTL lapses, so data written there can silently disappear. Keys that look like configuration, balances, or ownership are strong hints that the value is meant to persist. This is a key-name heuristic and reports low confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Durable data written to temporary storage\n\nTemporary storage entries are removed when their TTL lapses, so data written there can silently disappear. Keys that look like configuration, balances, or ownership are strong hints that the value is meant to persist. This is a key-name heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\n### Remediation\n\nWrite durable state to persistent (or instance) storage. Use temporary storage only for data that may safely be discarded, and extend its TTL when it is still needed.\n\n### References\n\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/state-archival",
                "text": "Temporary storage entries are removed when their TTL lapses, so data written there can silently disappear. Keys that look like configuration, balances, or ownership are strong hints that the value is meant to persist. This is a key-name heuristic and reports low confidence.\n\nRemediation: Write durable state to persistent (or instance) storage. Use temporary storage only for data that may safely be discarded, and extend its TTL when it is still needed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
              "id": "SS-021",
              "name": "Durable data written to temporary storage",
              "properties": {
                "category": "storage",
                "confidence": "low",
                "references": [
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/state-archival"
                ],
                "remediation": "Write durable state to persistent (or instance) storage. Use temporary storage only for data that may safely be discarded, and extend its TTL when it is still needed.",
                "severity": "medium"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Durable data written to temporary storage"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A contract uses `symbol_short!` with a literal longer than the nine-character short-symbol limit, or builds the symbol from caller-controlled input. Overlong literals do not describe the symbol the author expects once truncated or fail to compile, while caller-built short symbols can deliberately collide with another key and overwrite or misread storage. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports medium confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Unsafe symbol_short key\n\nA contract uses `symbol_short!` with a literal longer than the nine-character short-symbol limit, or builds the symbol from caller-controlled input. Overlong literals do not describe the symbol the author expects once truncated or fail to compile, while caller-built short symbols can deliberately collide with another key and overwrite or misread storage. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports medium confidence.\n\n### Remediation\n\nUse a constant literal of at most nine characters such as `symbol_short!(\"transfer\")`, or use `Symbol::new(&env, value)` for longer or dynamic values. Never derive a storage key directly from caller input; map it through an allowlist of known keys.\n\n### References\n\n- https://docs.rs/soroban-sdk/latest/soroban_sdk/macro.symbol_short.html\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security",
                "text": "A contract uses `symbol_short!` with a literal longer than the nine-character short-symbol limit, or builds the symbol from caller-controlled input. Overlong literals do not describe the symbol the author expects once truncated or fail to compile, while caller-built short symbols can deliberately collide with another key and overwrite or misread storage. This is a syntactic heuristic and reports medium confidence.\n\nRemediation: Use a constant literal of at most nine characters such as `symbol_short!(\"transfer\")`, or use `Symbol::new(&env, value)` for longer or dynamic values. Never derive a storage key directly from caller input; map it through an allowlist of known keys."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.rs/soroban-sdk/latest/soroban_sdk/macro.symbol_short.html",
              "id": "SS-022",
              "name": "Unsafe symbol_short key",
              "properties": {
                "category": "storage",
                "confidence": "medium",
                "references": [
                  "https://docs.rs/soroban-sdk/latest/soroban_sdk/macro.symbol_short.html",
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security"
                ],
                "remediation": "Use a constant literal of at most nine characters such as `symbol_short!(\"transfer\")`, or use `Symbol::new(&env, value)` for longer or dynamic values. Never derive a storage key directly from caller input; map it through an allowlist of known keys.",
                "severity": "medium"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unsafe symbol_short key"
              }
            },
